WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.195
Idea. A pie chart is a circle cut into slices, like a pizza. Here the whole circle is all the workers in a country. Each slice is the share working in one sector.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:24.883
In a developed country, such as Japan, T is the biggest slice, well over half. S comes second, about a quarter. P is only a thin slice. These sizes are an example, not real figures.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:34.152
In a developing country, such as Nepal, P is the biggest slice, and it must be at least half of the circle. S and T share the rest.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:45.609
Method. Step one, decide if the country is developed or developing. Step two, draw a line through the centre, straight up and down, to split the circle in half.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:57.679
Step three, size the slices. Developed: T more than half, S about a quarter, P under a tenth. Developing: P more than half, then split the rest between S and T.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:02.162
Step four, write P, S and T clearly inside each slice.

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:18.733
Worked example. Here is a real question from June 2021. On a blank pie chart, draw and label the sizes of the primary, secondary and tertiary sectors for a developing economy such as Haiti. Three marks.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:28.203
Haiti is a developing economy, so most people work in farming and other primary jobs. P must be the biggest slice, and more than half.

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:38.795
Draw the half-way line. Give P the whole of one half plus a bit more, about sixty per cent. Split the rest: T about thirty per cent, S about ten.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:59.665
Label each slice. Sixty plus thirty plus ten makes one hundred per cent, the whole circle. With a protractor, each one per cent is three point six degrees, so P is two hundred and sixteen degrees, but rough sizes are fine. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:10.431
Common mistake. A common mistake is making P the biggest slice, but less than half. For a developing country, P must reach the half-way line.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:18.645
Another is drawing three equal slices, or forgetting the labels. Make the size order clear, and label every slice.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:30.863
Your turn. Now you try. On a blank pie chart, draw and label the sizes of the three sectors for a developed economy such as Canada. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:43.716
Answer. Canada is developed, so T is the biggest slice, about seventy-five per cent. S is about twenty, and P is a thin slice of about five. That makes one hundred.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:53.819
Remember: decide developed or developing, halve the circle, size the slices, and label P, S and T. Now try the practice questions.
